No. Connecting the repo only gives ArgoCD permission to read it. It doesn't deploy or discover anything until you create an Application.

What ArgoCD does and doesn't do

	•	It does not scan the cluster and adopt what is already running.
	•	It only manages what's defined in Git for that Application (repo, branch, path, destination cluster and namespace).
	•	It compares those rendered manifests with the cluster, and it knows about live resources only if they match something in Git.

What happens when you create the Application

	•	Resources that are in Git and already exist in the cluster (same name and namespace): ArgoCD adopts them and shows a diff if they differ. A sync overwrites the live version with Git.
	•	Resources that are in Git but missing in the cluster: ArgoCD shows them as Missing and creates them on sync.
	•	Resources running in the cluster but not in Git (for example the manual MySQL StatefulSet, Service and PVC): ArgoCD ignores them. They show up only if pruning is on and they carry ArgoCD's tracking label, which unmanaged resources won't have. So they are neither deleted nor managed.

